::ENG2_U02_L06_GP_Q01::[MLA.ENG2.R.11] Source: A messenger shares restricted fire with people who need warmth and protection. Adaptation: Lina shares a guide to a restricted digital archive so classmates can study. Question: What element is preserved in both texts?{
=A. A character shares a restricted resource.#Correct. Both the messenger and Lina share something that was controlled by a smaller group.
~B. A character builds a mountain.#Incorrect. The mountain appears only in the source, and no character builds it.
~C. A character refuses to help anyone.#Incorrect. Both central characters help others.
~D. A character hides all knowledge forever.#Incorrect. Both texts focus on sharing knowledge or resources.
}

::ENG2_U02_L06_GP_Q02::[MLA.ENG2.R.11] Source: Fire is kept high on a mountain. Adaptation: A digital archive is restricted by a password. Question: What is transformed in the adaptation?{
~A. The mountain becomes a forest.#Incorrect. The adaptation changes the setting to a school, not a forest.
=B. Fire becomes access to digital information.#Correct. The adaptation changes the powerful resource from fire to a digital archive.
~C. The messenger becomes a cook.#Incorrect. Lina is a student technology captain, not a cook.
~D. The people become animals.#Incorrect. The adaptation keeps the focus on people, specifically students.
}

::ENG2_U02_L06_GP_Q03::[MLA.ENG2.R.11] Source: The messenger is punished for disobeying. Adaptation: Lina shares a guide to the archive. Question: What consequence does Lina face?{
~A. She wins a mountain race.#Incorrect. No race appears in the adaptation.
~B. She loses the archive forever.#Incorrect. The passage says more students learn to use the archive.
=C. She loses her captain position.#Correct. Lina loses her technology captain position after sharing the guide.
~D. She is given fire.#Incorrect. Fire belongs to the source, not the adaptation.
}

::ENG2_U02_L06_GP_Q04::[MLA.ENG2.R.11] Source: Fire helps people survive. Adaptation: The digital archive helps students research and study. Question: What is the effect of changing fire into a digital archive?{
~A. It makes the story about cooking.#Incorrect. The archive is connected to research and study, not cooking.
~B. It removes the idea of shared knowledge.#Incorrect. The adaptation emphasizes shared knowledge.
~C. It makes Lina identical to the mountain keeper.#Incorrect. Lina is more like the messenger because she shares the resource.
=D. It makes the old conflict relevant to modern learning.#Correct. The adaptation updates the powerful resource so it fits a school setting.
}

::ENG2_U02_L06_GP_Q05::[MLA.ENG2.R.11] Source: A messenger shares restricted fire. Adaptation: Lina shares a restricted archive guide. Question: Which response is strongest?{
~A. The two texts are different.#Incorrect. This is true but too vague because it does not explain the adaptation.
=B. Lina shares a restricted archive just as the messenger shares restricted fire, so the adaptation keeps the theme of access to knowledge.#Correct. This compares both texts and explains the preserved idea.
~C. The adaptation has a school.#Incorrect. This names a setting but does not analyze adaptation.
~D. The source has a mountain.#Incorrect. This names a detail but does not explain how the adaptation works.
}
